WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Welcome back to Models and Agents episode 88 for June 21st, 2026.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.980
Their signal and noise in AI every day, let's get to the signal.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.880
The week's biggest developments pulled together,

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.880
what actually moved, why it matters, and what to watch next.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.020
Z.AI shipped GLM 5.2 on June 13th

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.860
with a 1 million token context window

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.000
and two explicit thinking effort modes called high and max.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.020
The model routes through anthropic compatible endpoints,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.280
so it drops straight into clawed code,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.100
climb an open claw with no code changes.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.540
No benchmarks appeared at launch,

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.040
yet MIT licensed open weights are promised for the following week.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.160
Teams already running those coding tools can swap the endpoint today

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.820
and measure whether the extra context helps multi-file work.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.040
Remember, we last checked frontier models on episode 86.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.880
This release adds an open weight contender

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.060
that directly tests the cost versus reasoning trade-off

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.640
the category has been tracking.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.720
Open AI published deployment simulation research

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.820
that replays de-identified past conversations

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.260
through candidate models

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.420
to estimate real failure rates before release.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.400
The method produced a 1.5x median multiplicative error

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.180
on held out deployment data

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.060
and extends to multi-turn agent scenarios

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.940
by simulating tool calls.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.320
Traditional static benchmarks remain necessary

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.320
for rare high severity cases

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.560
while this approach supplies frequency estimates

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.220
under realistic conditions.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.000
Anthropic reported that clawed code task value

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.360
rose 27% over six months

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.240
even as success rates held steady across domains.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.560
Microsoft research introduced next latent prediction

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.540
which layers compact world model prediction

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.080
on top of standard next token training

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.420
and yields up to 3.3 times faster inference.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:05.340
Open AI released life side bench 750 expert authored tasks

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.160
spanning seven biological research workflows

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.120
created with 173 scientists.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.140
GPT Rosalind outperformed GPT 5.5

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.160
on every workflow in the suite

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.740
and GPT 5.4 completed a full medicinal chemistry project

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.860
from literature to validated result

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.320
when paired with molecule.1's Maria AI.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.380
Nvidia released NimaTron 3 Ultra

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:36.060
an open frontier model built as a 550 billion slash 55 billion

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.260
mixture of experts hybrid mamba architecture

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.620
with 1 million token context.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:45.020
The model targets sustained agent operation

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.300
rather than single turn chat addressing the gap

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.580
where most open models lose coherence across long tool use loops.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.780
Remember we covered open weight models on episode 85.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:00.740
NimaTron 3 Ultra gives builders a self hostable option

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.940
that competes on agent relevant tasks

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.920
without closed model restrictions.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.020
Deepseek previewed its V4 series of mixed

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.340
mixture of experts models that support million token context

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.100
with major efficiency gains over prior releases.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.640
A developer open sourced a CUDA kernel

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.440
that keeps top K vector search entirely on the GPU.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.240
Removing the PsiE roundtrip

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.480
that previously created variable latency

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.020
in agentic retrieval augmented generation.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.040
The kernel produces deterministic microsecond scale

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.800
tail latencies while preserving answer quality

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.940
which matters for production agents

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.200
that must meet strict response times service level agreements.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.680
Virtual's wired latens distributed GPU engine into its agent network

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.680
so agents can call GLM 5.2 across a shared compute fabric

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.000
without managing individual clusters.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.280
Teams already running virtual agents gain immediate access

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.300
to the new model without fresh infrastructure work.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.460
Simon Willison documented how fabled files refusal patterns

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.280
under export control rules now blocked the multi-step fix this code

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.140
prompts defenders use daily on vulnerable open source examples.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.840
The model rejected an initial review for security issues framing

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.540
yet complied when asked simply to fix,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.200
turning the output into working test scripts.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.500
Remember, we tracked safety and policy on episode 87.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.700
This enforcement treats routine defensive patching

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:37.040
as a prohibited capability and narrow access to models

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.740
that support the fine fixed test loop inside the United States.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.520
Hermes agent added asynchronous sub agents that no longer block the

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.500
parent session during delegated work.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.560
The update appears in the same week as Nemotron 3 Ultra

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.380
and Fable 5 Export Control coverage.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.320
Mark Tech Post reported the change on June 16th.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.900
Builders testing long-running agent workflows can now

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.500
delegate tasks without halting the main chat thread.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:08.020
The feature directly addresses one of the open questions

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.620
on agent reliability tracks since episode 87.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.860
Watch for integration examples that combine the new sub agent behavior

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.360
with million token context models released this week.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.720
ViveThinker 3B is a 3 billion parameter dense model derived from

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.800
quen 2.5 colder 3B through the spectrum to signal post-training pipeline.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:37.320
It reaches parity with deep seek v3.2 and k2.5 on verifiable reasoning

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.940
benchmarks under an MIT license that permits commercial use and fine tuning.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:51.780
Liquid AI introduced LFM 2-5 embedding 350M and LFM 2-5 colbert 3-5-0M

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.940
dense biocoder and late interaction models for fast multi-lingual search.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:03.200
The pair covers 11 languages and targets retrieval workloads that appear inside agent loops.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:07.660
Mark Tech Post covered the release on June 19th.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:13.180
These embedding models can be paired with the new GPU resident top k kernel

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.140
to keep both indexing and search on device.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.760
Teams running retrieval augmented generation should test whether the late interaction variant

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:27.320
reduces context leakage compared with prior biocoder baselines.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:33.180
Google Cloud introduced the Open Knowledge Format, a vendor neutral markdown specification

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.300
that supplies curated context to AI agents.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:42.580
The format was announced the same week as GLM.5.2 and point availability.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.540
It gives agent builders a structured way to inject domain knowledge without vendor lock-in.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:54.620
Early adopters can convert existing documentation repositories into OKF files

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.360
and serve them alongside the new 1 million token models.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:05.520
Mark Tech Post noted the release on June 16th, a major security report detailed active exploits

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:12.360
against Langflow and related agent frameworks. The findings were referenced in episode 87 coverage

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.320
and remain relevant for any self-hosted deployment.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:22.280
Teams should audit running instances this week before connecting new models such as NEMOTRON-3 Ultra

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:29.400
or GLM times 5.2. The report underscores the ongoing safety and policy thread

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:33.700
that has followed export control and evaluation discussions throughout the month.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.020
VentureBeat published analysis showing that fine-tuning forgets prior knowledge

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:45.400
while rag leaks context and that hypernetworks can build the model and agent needs on demand.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.780
The piece appeared on June 20th, hypernetworks offer a third path that avoids both permanent

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:58.060
weight changes and repeated retrieval steps. Builders working on long horizon agents can prototype

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:02.520
the approach against the Atomic Fact Memory Systems also released this week.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:10.300
A new GPU resident top-k kernel and Atomic Fact Memory Systems for agents both surfaced in

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:16.520
research this week offering concrete ways to reduce tail latency and context leakage in long

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:23.560
horizon workflows. If you run retrieval augmented agents benchmark the open-sourced Kudah

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:29.500
kernel against your current retrieval step this week. If you already use Cloud Code or Cline,

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:38.340
point the endpoint at GLM-5.2 and test the high versus max effort modes on a multi-file refactoring

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:47.140
task. If you build life science tooling run GPT Rosalind and GPT 5.4 against the life-side bench

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:55.020
tasks that match your domain. If you self-host agents audit any langflow or related framework instances

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:02.740
for the active exploits detailed in this week's security report. Keep an eye on the GLM-5.2

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:09.500
open weights release and any early independent comparisons of its effort tiers on real workloads.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.780
Watch whether regulators issue clarification on whether routine code review prompts remain

